At a feasible point \( ar x\), the linear independence constraint qualification holds if the family \[\{
abla h_j( ar x):j=1,\ldots,p\}\cup\{
abla g_i( ar x):i\in I( ar x)\}\] is linearly independent. Equivalently, \[\sum_{j=1}^p\mu_j
abla h_j( ar x)+\sum_{i\in I( ar x)}\lambda_i
abla g_i( ar x)=0\] implies \(\mu=0\) and \(\lambda_i=0\) for every \(i\in I( ar x)\). LICQ is stronger than MFCQ and ensures uniqueness of KKT multipliers when they exist.

Let \(I( ar x)=\{i:g_i( ar x)=0\}\). The constant positive-linear-dependence qualification holds at the feasible point \( ar x\) if there exists a neighborhood \(U\) of \( ar x\) such that, for every \(J\subset I( ar x)\), whenever there are multipliers \(\lambda_i\ge0\), \(i\in J\), and \(\mu_j\in\mathbb{R}\), \(j=1,\ldots,p\), not all zero, satisfying \[\sum_{i\in J}\lambda_i
abla g_i( ar x)+\sum_{j=1}^p\mu_j
abla h_j( ar x)=0,\] the family \[\{
abla g_i(x):i\in J\}\cup\{
abla h_j(x):j=1,\ldots,p\}\] is linearly dependent for every \(x\in U\).
